WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.290
Professor Noveck
is Florence Rogatz

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.990
visiting professor and
director of The Governance Lab

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.390
at Yale Law school.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.880
She's the Jerry Hultin
Global Network Professor

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.970
at New York University's
Tandon School of Engineering

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:31.330
and director of the GovLab
lab and its MacArthur Research

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.610
Network on opening governance.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.750
Professor Noveck
focuses her research,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.890
teaching, and activism on
the impact of technology

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.400
on public institutions and
solving public problems.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.670
A graduate of Harvard
University and Yale Law School--

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.150
welcome home-- she is a
member of the advisory board

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.430
of the open
contracting partnership

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.970
and is program committee
member of the 2016

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.710
conferences on
international open data,

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.700
open data research, data
science for government,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.950
and the data for good exchange.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.400
Welcome.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:04.950
Thank you.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.325
Let's see, do I
have a deck here?

00:01:06.325 --> 00:01:09.146
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:12.920
No, no, no.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.180
We'll just change that.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.507
I have this for other reasons.

00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:18.090
I'll tell you what, I'll start
while if you don't mind bring--

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:18.810
no.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.680
I'll let you put my deck
up and that way we'll--

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.400
so I am-- let me start--

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.710
it's under Noveck, I
hope, or somewhere--

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.210
by saying I am
delighted to be here.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.340
And I'm here first
and foremost just

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.180
to meet the people in
the room and to enjoy

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.510
the fellowship of people
who have so much in common.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.260
And also to report--

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.070
because it wasn't on the
list that was up earlier--

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.710
that we now have an open data
clinic at Yale Law School

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.650
as well, where we
have lots of students

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.260
working on and eager
to work on open data

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.240
related policy projects.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.150
So I'm here a little bit
as the lawyer in the room--

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.810
don't all now leave
at once-- but to talk

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.680
about the legal and
ethical and policy side

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.509
of some of this work.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:11.240
Again, there's a
lot that has already

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.770
been covered so I will
try not to repeat unduly.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.050
But I want to pick up
on a point that was

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.510
made earlier perhaps in jest.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.430
So I was the head
of open government

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.440
and open data at
OSTP, so you have

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.180
me to blame for some of
those unfunded mandates.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.720
And the throw away
comment that you

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.640
made about the end of open
science and open government

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.136
and open data in
the White House,

00:02:37.136 --> 00:02:39.260
I want to make sure to
bring that back to the fore.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:43.130
Because though it was funny, as
we know it's really not funny.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.830
And so I want to make sure
that we're at least focused on

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.750
and the one thing that
we take away from this

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:51.260
is that as much as we're
excited about the technologies

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:55.820
of big data, as much as we're
eager to talk as we will today,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.380
and as the students
showed yesterday

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.320
about the many important things
we can do with the data that's

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.640
available, it's not simply
the tools that are making

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.320
possible everything
we're talking about,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:07.970
it's the policies
that underlie them.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.250
And that obviously runs
through the conversation.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.280
So it's the fact that we now
have a policy of open data

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.320
at the national level
in the United States

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.210
and that next week,
70 countries will

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:20.240
meet for the Open Government
Partnership in Paris.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.960
70 countries who have
signed on to commitments

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.290
to open up their data, to create
data portals at the national,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.450
at the state, at
the local level.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.730
15 of those countries
have now signed on

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:34.880
to an international
open data charter which

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.840
they are making commitments--

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.840
what I'm looking for is
the exact language which

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.730
I won't find at the moment--

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.570
in which they are going to
make commitments to essentially

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:47.600
publish all data
in digital format,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.330
downloadable, open to
the public a apriori.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.270
So, we still have a long way
to go to realize the vision,

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:56.420
but at least we have the
cornerstone in terms of policy

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.210
that started with President
Obama's memorandum

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.250
on his very first day
in office, a memorandum

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:05.540
on transparency and open
government, in which he said

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.680
that data data collected
by the government, paid

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.060
for by our taxpayer dollars,
collected in the government's

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.554
practice as regulator and
as researcher belongs to us.

00:04:18.554 --> 00:04:20.720
It was very controversial,
I might add, at the time.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.630
It seems obvious to us now,
but it was very controversial,

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:25.820
the idea that we
weren't simply focusing

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.410
on data about the
workings of government,

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.650
such as the money that
it spends or the salaries

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:31.910
of public officials.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:34.520
But that in fact we would try
to open up all the data that

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.430
could power things
like the innovations

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.200
that we are talking about today.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.260
And then going beyond the
idea of just a policy,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:46.630
of just words, the creation
of platforms that actually

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:49.270
allow us to put data somewhere.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.360
To have a place for people
to find that data so that we

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.280
can go beyond the
promise and make

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.560
the practice real in principle.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.330
There's great things
that it's allowing

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.160
us to do in terms
of transforming

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.210
how government works.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.910
So when they put up the
school spending data in Mexico

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.790
on Mejora Tu Escuela,
we can see right away

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:10.870
that 1512 people
all on the payroll,

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:12.460
all have the same birthdate.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:15.130
They're all 102 years
old and they all

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.710
make more than the
president of Mexico.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.550
So it allows us to yes, improve
government accountability,

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:24.970
but it also in the end allows
parents to make better choices.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.710
And ultimately, it's not
a story about the data

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:29.650
and it's not a story
about the platforms,

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.140
even as we know and as
you so well pointed out,

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:34.780
it's about the communities
that we build around the data.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.720
So in Rajasthan in
India, the group MKSS

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:41.170
has for the last decade been
taking government spending

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:45.220
data, budget data, and painting
it on the walls of villages.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.770
And holding readings,
theatrical performances,

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.320
in which they read the budget.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.920
If that doesn't sound exciting,
I don't know what does.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:54.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.830
But what they do is
then they ask people

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:58.990
where is the bridge
built to nowhere?

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.960
Where is the person who's died
who's still on the payroll?

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.620
And through that mechanism,
using data as the raw material,

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:10.270
co-create the process
and the practice

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:13.630
of greater accountability
in government.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:15.610
So I want to just spend
a couple of minutes

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.430
on talking about another
sort of development.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.530
You're all very familiar
with kind of the movement

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.180
around open data.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.760
The one, though, that I want
to emphasize that we have

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.640
to continue to fight for,
we cannot, especially now,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.550
take it as a given or
take it for granted,

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.692
the two leading countries,
the US and the UK,

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:34.900
that spearheaded this movement
have both had a change

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:38.530
of administration, neither of
which seem poised to invest

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:39.700
in this agenda.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.730
That's a best case scenario
is that they ignore it.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.550
But we all know
that the reality is

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.430
that we might see significant
rollbacks on the opening

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:50.290
up of data and
investments in open data

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.720
since it costs real
money to actually do it.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.430
What we are seeing,
though, is a rise

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:58.540
of what many people call social
corporate data philanthropy

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.030
or what we call
data collaboratives.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.310
Bloomberg just had a
big conference on data

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:08.200
science for social good in which
many companies came, committing

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.569
to open up their data.

00:07:09.569 --> 00:07:11.860
And we know that part of the
clinical trial story, part

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.620
of what so many people in this
room have been fighting for,

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.560
is to not only get the
clinical trials data from NIH,

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:21.250
but to also get it directly
from the companies that

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.320
are producing this data.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.470
So, it's Twitter providing
its fire hose of data

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.650
to the University of Wollongong
in Australia and then using

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:34.240
that, the university using that
to create flood maps to help

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.390
promote resilience-- excuse me--

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:39.220
in Indonesia.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:43.980
So we're seeing lots of ways in
which open data, including data

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:47.490
that comes from government, data
that comes from researchers,

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:51.330
data that comes from companies,
through collaboration often

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:52.672
between these three parties--

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:54.380
that's what we call
data collaboratives--

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:57.270
it's at that intersection
that a lot of exciting work

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:58.187
is happening.

00:07:58.187 --> 00:08:00.270
I only have a few minutes
today to talk about this

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.730
so I would point you to a
website that we've created

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.970
called OD Impact,
Open Data's Impact,

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:09.960
where we're collecting case
studies and in-depth stories

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.500
but also that analysis of
these examples of open data.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.540
We have another
project with USAID

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.560
focused on that work in the
development context as well.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:20.730
That's all going up here.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:23.479
And I might add to your point,
this is fully open source

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:25.020
and replicable and
a number of people

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.720
have already forked this to
create their own archives

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.490
and repositories of open
data impact stories.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.730
Since so much of this
work, as you said earlier,

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.429
is about telling
the story of impact,

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:37.620
not just talking
about the promise.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.600
So I want to fly through
a couple of examples

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.090
that again, I think for
you, this is a room that

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:43.797
needs no convincing.

00:08:43.797 --> 00:08:45.630
But I think it's important
to at least share

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:47.580
a little bit of the
story about the ways

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:50.190
in which we are transforming
not only science,

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.380
but we're transforming
government and other sectors

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.910
through open data.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.100
And then to end with a
couple of just thoughts

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:57.750
on the policy side of this.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:00.060
So obviously, we can
do lots of great stuff

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:04.680
now in terms of our ability
to provide services,

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:08.430
especially to vulnerable people,
by monitoring populations

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.550
in real time.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:11.940
So when we open up things
like the National Oceanic

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:15.060
and Atmospheric data,
we can do things

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:17.040
like better predict the weather.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.020
And a lot of that data,
though, some of it's

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.690
coming from the government
as we know, but some of that

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.440
data also then comes
from companies.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:25.860
So it's Intel opening up
data that collects off

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.990
of sensors that it's using.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.030
And the combination
of data is allowing

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:34.500
us to create new tools that
help to promote resilience.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.890
We can, as we saw with the
Mejora Tu Escuela example,

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.520
also improve how government
spends money and government

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.560
efficiency and
government effectiveness.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.810
As was mentioned, I'm on
the board of a nonprofit

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.820
called the Open
Contracting Partnership

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.770
which works with
governments around the world

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:54.060
to open up their contracting
and spending data.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.360
By the way, the data is open
but we need people to look at it

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.730
and use it if we're going to
actually spot the fraud, waste,

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:01.399
and abuse.

00:10:01.399 --> 00:10:02.940
At the very least,
what it's starting

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.510
to do is to decrease a
culture of corruption

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:10.170
in places like Slovakia, the
Ukraine, which interestingly

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.770
is very out ahead in last
year on open contracting data,

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.020
and Mexico and elsewhere.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.150
But we're seeing this
move towards government

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.540
using its own data to
actually decrease its spending

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.982
and become more accountable.

00:10:23.982 --> 00:10:25.690
We're seeing then, of
course, the ability

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:28.720
to use this data to solve
major social problems.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.150
So we have problems
hiding in plain sight

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.880
like that in Zanesville,
Ohio where when you actually

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.060
scrutinize the data, you
discover that clean water is

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:37.810
being delivered.

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.187
There's a red lining problem.

00:10:39.187 --> 00:10:41.020
If you live in a
neighborhood that is white,

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:43.167
you're receiving
clean city water.

00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:45.250
If you live in a neighborhood
that's traditionally

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:47.680
African-American, you are not.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.050
This led to then a
lawsuit, a change

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:54.280
in the situation, damages
that were paid to the victims.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:57.010
But it would not have been
possible without open data

00:10:57.010 --> 00:11:00.550
about city water services,
about city water practices that

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.830
allow us then to spot
this kind of unfairness

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:05.968
and to rectify major
social problems.

00:11:05.968 --> 00:11:08.740
And so we're also
seeing, obviously, lots

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:10.414
of use of data collection.

00:11:10.414 --> 00:11:11.830
And this is
particularly important

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.630
where citizen science
comes in, in terms

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.870
of using people on the
ground as data collectors.

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:20.890
We're seeing incidentally a
lot of that use of citizens

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:23.200
as data collectors
now as monitors

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:26.920
of rises of hate incidents
and bias incidents,

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:29.500
as you've seen lots of
efforts by different groups

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:32.740
to collect data about
hate crimes and rises

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.570
of incidents of hate
that are occurring.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:38.500
So the collection of data,
particularly by citizens,

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.350
that we can use when made
open as Christchurch,

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:44.710
New Zealand did to be able then
to figure out which buildings

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:47.650
are about to fall down, where
can people move back in,

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:50.140
how do we actually
rebuild the city?

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:53.380
So the CIO of
Christchurch, New Zealand

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:56.350
was in fact very out
ahead on using open data.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.300
And then that collaboration
and partnership

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.490
with citizens, both
to collect the data

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.910
but also to scrutinize the data
to be able to develop solutions

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.580
in real time.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.880
And again, what you see is a
lot of this work happening now

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.010
at the intersection
between government

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:13.480
and private companies.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:16.750
Because especially in a lot
of developing world contexts,

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.630
there is no administrative
state infrastructure.

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:22.750
There isn't that data being
collected by government.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.660
And so, we need to depend on the
work of groups like Real Impact

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:28.870
Analytics and Twitter and
others who have the data

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.060
and push them to
make it available so

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.880
that we can do this
kind of social good work

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:35.770
more efficiently
and effectively.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:37.600
My lab has a
partnership with UNICEF.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.120
Over the next year and a
half, we'll be working--

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:42.610
we have both work to
define the problems

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.300
that UNICEF works on in terms
of improving children's lives.

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:49.300
And then trying to find the
partners that we can turn to

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:52.990
in countries that don't have a
census, that don't have a data

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:55.450
collection infrastructure,
or where we might A,

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.880
create one using
citizen scientists or B,

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:00.930
use data that companies
are anyway collecting.

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:03.100
Finally, the last example
that I want to give,

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:05.080
and it's an example
that we frequently

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:08.680
use when we have a hard time
convincing people of the need

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:11.530
to open up their data, we can
see how it works with provosts.

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.960
I know how it works with
prime ministers, which

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.540
is you tell them not that open
data is the right thing to do,

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:18.640
you don't tell them about
solving social problems

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:21.520
and helping flood victims
and babies and children,

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:24.640
you tell them it'll create jobs
and promote economic growth.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:27.580
You tell them about how New
York City is using open data

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:30.310
to create a business atlas
that gives the small business

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:32.230
owner the same kind
of market research

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:34.300
information about where
to site their jewelry

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:38.980
store or their coffee shop as
the Starbucks or the Gap has.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:41.562
We have a project called
the Open Data 500, which

00:13:41.562 --> 00:13:43.270
has been tracking--
and this, by the way,

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:44.914
Open Data 500 is
in the US and then

00:13:44.914 --> 00:13:46.330
we have a bunch
of other countries

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.330
that we're partnered with
labs in other places--

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:52.902
tracking businesses that use
open data to create jobs.

00:13:52.902 --> 00:13:55.360
And we're working with scholars
at Columbia Business School

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.120
to track the investment
in those businesses.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:01.390
And their research shows
that in fact, businesses

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:04.930
driven by open data, perhaps
surprisingly, in fact

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:08.920
receive no less investment than
people who used closed assets

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:10.090
and closed resources.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:11.830
In fact, they do just as well.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.770
And there's a tremendous number
of exciting stories about how

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:16.900
companies-- and back to
your point about creating

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:19.690
the commercial layer on
top of these services--

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:22.490
the fact that in this country,
unlike in many others,

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:26.030
we have a policy which says
you can reuse the data,

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:30.670
including for commercial
purposes, helps to create jobs.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:32.590
So let me just-- let
me sort of wind up.

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:34.561
I have no idea what time it is.

00:14:34.561 --> 00:14:35.810
We have a couple more minutes.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:39.767
So, ultimately, I think
the biggest benefiT--

00:14:39.767 --> 00:14:41.350
and we know this
from the science case

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:43.360
but we also see it as
much in the government

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:46.570
case, both in terms
of reproducibility

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:48.130
in all of these
contexts-- is what

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:51.230
we do is we get more
eyeballs on a problem.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:53.590
So by opening up the
data, we can then

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.890
get-- which one is this one-- we
can then get election watchers.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:58.870
We can then create a
crowdsourcing project

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:02.500
as the Kawal Pemilu project
did, using open election

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.120
data to then--

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.070
in a project that I
might add cost something

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:10.060
on the order of $57 and
was done in about a week.

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:12.321
These stories are all so
wonderful about how cheap

00:15:12.321 --> 00:15:12.820
they are.

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:16.270
We're then able to
scrutinize election results

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.050
for fraud, et cetera.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:21.160
So let me-- I'm going to
skip ahead because I want

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:24.540
to talk about the challenges.

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:26.910
So how do we realize
this benefit,

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:28.470
whether in the
government context

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:29.880
or in the science context?

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.640
Obviously, we need
to continue to push.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:33.930
And that's part of the--

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:36.300
that's the entire impetus
for this event today.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:41.160
We need to push for the policies
against data hugging disorder,

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.350
against data hoarding disorder.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:45.780
But we have to do it
in all of these sectors

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:48.120
and we have to do it in
all of them in tandem

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:51.030
because we need the
collaboration between them

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:54.840
if we're to realize the social
benefits from having the data.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:57.900
So we need to keep pushing
on the government side,

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:00.630
but we need to also do
this work with companies,

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.580
with research
institutes, et cetera,

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:06.480
in order to realize
these benefits.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:10.470
It's not, though, all a
story about open data, right?

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:13.020
And we know very well,
particularly for people

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:14.910
who are doing human
subjects research, who

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:16.950
are doing biomedical
research, this

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:21.450
is also a story about
the Fifty Shades of Gray

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:23.670
in between closed
and open, namely,

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:26.670
responsible data access
and data governance.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:30.990
Knowing how to share data in
reliable and responsible ways.

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.360
So I was very proud that
my students yesterday

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:35.850
got to present a poster
on work that they're

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:40.950
doing with two jurisdictions now
in Lafayette Parish, Louisiana

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:45.270
and Codington County, South
Dakota on their governance

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.090
practices for sharing data
between their criminal justice

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:49.710
and mental health agencies.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:53.130
Helping them to work
through that process

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.200
and develop a tool
kit and kind of a map

00:16:55.200 --> 00:17:00.240
for how to do that so they can
in turn reduce incarceration

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:03.600
by diverting the mentally
ill out of jail or prison

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.220
and into treatment.

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:07.680
That can only be done
through data sharing.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.180
But that has to be
done in ways that

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:12.660
are legally compliant, that
are ethically compliant,

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:15.569
where they actually know
what IT to use unlike one

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:17.730
of the jurisdictions
which simply emails

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:21.270
a spreadsheet of everybody from
one agency to another, which

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:25.130
we know is not the
best way to do it.

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:27.060
So there's a whole
range of issues

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.120
that intertwine the
technical and the legal

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:33.450
and the ethical that are really
questions of data governance

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.460
that are at the center
of a lot of the work

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:36.870
that we see happening.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:42.240
There's fabulous work being
done now by the UN's Food Lab

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:44.400
and the UN hunger
program collecting

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:48.540
data about Syrian refugees
and their consumption of food

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:49.410
in the camps.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:52.355
But they can't simply publish
and visualize this data

00:17:52.355 --> 00:17:53.730
lest they make
the people they're

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.970
trying to help potential
targets and victims of crime

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:58.230
and attack.

00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:00.540
So there are lots of
difficult ethical questions

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.310
that people are debating.

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:04.440
And in this country
where we have launched--

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:07.980
we announced it last year
and about 120 years days ago,

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:11.730
the checks were dispersed in the
Precision Medicine Initiative--

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.770
we have this great
hope that we will

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:18.450
start to collect longitudinal
data from a million people

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.280
not just about their
genetics, but now

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:23.130
about their cultural practices,
their environmental practices,

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:24.730
what they do.

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:27.600
And to do that data, to
collect it effectively,

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:30.240
we have to know responsibly
how to handle it.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.400
It's great if we
have 10 years of data

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:35.520
on people that can allow us
to quickly spot that I might

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:37.560
be at the risk of diabetes.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:40.980
But it's terrible if by
virtue of that surveillance,

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:44.040
I suddenly start getting
coupons for Big Macs.

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:45.840
Then we're doing
something wrong.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:47.880
We're doing something
wrong if we're not then

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:51.540
also putting the people at
the center of the process

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:53.310
and ensuring that
in that research,

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:56.490
we are prioritizing the
needs and the conditions

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.320
of the people who
are most underserved

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:01.380
and underrepresented
in biomedical research.

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:04.290
I'm glad to report that in the
Precision Medicine Initiative,

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.050
they have taken extra steps
to create a national level

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:11.370
IRB with a high degree of
diversity in that IRB to ensure

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:14.190
exactly that, that
people and their privacy

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:16.045
is being put at the center.

00:19:16.045 --> 00:19:17.670
So let me stop--
because I want to make

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.470
sure we have some
time for questions--

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:22.860
and just say that as we talk
about all the fabulous things

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:26.400
that we can do with big
data in the sciences,

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:31.290
that we must put the policies
of privacy, of openness,

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:34.410
of ethical and responsible
sharing, and of governance

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:35.550
at the center.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:39.000
And so with all that, I
make a case for the lawyers

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.740
and why we're very happy to
be part of this conversation.

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:42.480
Thank you.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.330
[APPLAUSE]

